  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 143 AM CDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\n\nUpper level ridging will remain anchored across the southeast CONUS \nyet Saturday. The center of the upper high will gradually translate \nback westward into the desert southwest by early next week. ENS/GEFS \nmean PWAT values are very high yet Saturday, as \nmonsoonal/subtropical moisture aloft flows northeastward from \nwestern Mexico on the northwest periphery of the upper level high. \nENS continues to suggest PWAT values greater than 1.8\" (which would \nbe exceeding the the 90th percentile). A shortwave is forecast to \ntrack northeastward across the area Saturday. Showers and \nthunderstorms will be likely as a cold front sliding southward \nprovides a focus for convective development. Mid and upper flow \nsupports some training/backbuilding potential, which could localize \nheavy rainfall. The 12/12Z ENS mean QPF Saturday is around 0.3 to \n0.5\" across the area during the day Saturday. Will have to monitor \nwith such high water content in the atmosphere, as locally heavier \namounts will be possible where any training or backbuilding of \nconvection occurs. This seems most likely to occur across southwest \ninto central Nebraska where the front is forecast to be located by \nlate Saturday afternoon.  \n\nBy Sunday, as the southern plains upper high begins to migrate back \nwestward the flow aloft becomes more westerly. At the surface, weak \nCanadian high pressure builds into the area behind the cold front. \nPWAT values decrease some as the flow aloft turns more westerly, and \nappears convection will focus more south of the area across \nKansas near the cold front. \n\nAs the ridge becomes established across the desert southwest early \nnext week, flow aloft becomes more northwesterly. Although PWAT \nvalues decrease some, there will still be sufficient moisture both \nat the surface and aloft. This appears to be a set up for \nfor at least isolated southeastward tracking convection.   \n\nTemperatures will average below normal Saturday into the first of \nnext week. Highs will average in the mid 70s to mid 80s.",
